>
  <p>When we say the authoring path ends in a <strong>human-reviewed PR</strong>, we mean exactly that: a person still has to read the change and merge it. The skill is allowed to draft. It is not allowed to publish.</p>

  <h2>What the skill is allowed to do</h2>
  <p><code>traverse-capability-author</code> in <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/claude-skills/tree/main/skills/traverse-capability-author" target="_blank" rel="noopener">traverse-framework/claude-skills</a> can interview you, check the registry, write the contract, produce executable ABI-compliant WASM, and open a pull request into <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry" target="_blank" rel="noopener">traverse-framework/registry</a>. Under the hood that artifact is still Rust compiled to WASM. None of that bypasses review.</p>

  <h2>What a reviewer is actually checking</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Does the contract match the rule?</strong> Inputs, outputs, fail-closed cases, and placement should describe the business behavior you meant — not a vague stub.</li>
    <li><strong>Does CI pass?</strong> Registry PRs still hit coverage gates, digest checks, and the repo's pre-merge pipeline. Green checks are required; the skill does not waive them.</li>
    <li><strong>Are digests stable?</strong> <code>artifact.digest</code> / URL metadata has to survive review. Hand-edited digests usually fail for a reason.</li>
    <li><strong>Is this worth publishing?</strong> Prefer business-shaped rules agents should not free-hand (eligibility, pricing caps, authz, consent, risk, policy). Tiny utilities can teach the pipeline; they are not the ceiling. See the <a href="/blog/why-next-help-wanted-is-business-shaped.html">business-shaped help-wanted</a> write-up.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What human review is not</h2>
  <p>It is not a rubber stamp on whatever an LLM pasted. It is not a maintainer-only upload that skips the PR. It is not “CI green means automatically merged.” Fork-and-PR is the path; collaborator seats are not the first-publish door.</p>

  <h2>If you authored via the skill</h2>
  <p>Your job after the skill opens the PR is to read it like a reviewer would: confirm the contract text, skim the generated sources if you care, wait for CI, and answer review comments. Details of that handoff: <a href="/questions/how-do-i-go-from-skill-draft-to-published-capability.html">How do I go from a skill draft to a published capability?</a></p>
</QuestionLayout>
